[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says otherwise. The reports in it
describe three problems, each with its own fix, so each has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering update, 05 Oct: `parser` fixed (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`), `hint` won't fix (booking-v3 form retired).
Nothing has been done about the one-day moves: their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## The tickets

- **`parser`**, [#1](https://github.com/BartekButrym/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/1), fixed. The shared parser read dates month first;
  every tenant here writes the day first, so day and month swapped. The count is the `parser` row above.
- **`hint`**, [#2](https://github.com/BartekButrym/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/2), won't fix. The booking-v3 form printed `MM/DD/YYYY`
  under its date field and read the field day first. The table marks this row "not this ticket": it had no ticket of its own until now.
- **one-day move**, [#3](https://github.com/BartekButrym/mega-s01e03-kb/issues/3), open. The reports the desk read as a one-day
  move (the note under the table) describe a date one day off, or one meeting on two days in two cities. No swap of day and month
  explains that, so neither fix closes them. As the desk counts, they stay filed under `parser` by tenant.

## How the reports were read

- One report, one id: the export pages overlap, and the table counts each id once.
- Cause comes from the tenant and the channel (`causes.ts`), never from the intake tag, which guessed from the reporter's browser.

## Why they were merged

Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. Every one of these reports says some version
of "wrong date", so subject matching put three problems in one ticket.

